 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Precision:** Floating-point precision in matrix multiplications can affect ray direction accuracy, especially for distant objects [G].
-*   **Boundaries:** Pixel coordinates should be handled carefully to avoid off-by-one errors, especially when using `meshgrid` [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If the intrinsic matrix is singular, inversion will fail [G]. Ensure valid camera parameters.
+*   **Precision:** Floating-point precision in matrix inversions (e.g., $K^{-1}$) can affect ray direction accuracy, especially for wide-angle lenses [G].
+*   **Boundaries:** Pixel coordinates must be handled carefully to avoid off-by-one errors at image boundaries [G].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If the intrinsic matrix is singular, inversion will fail [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Ray Unification
-A technique to map every ray starting location onto a sphere circumscribed around the view cell, ensuring that identical rays have identical depth values regardless of the camera position, easing the task for the oracle network [S3]. For arbitrary views looking outside the view cell, map the ray origin to the circumscribed sphere. For 360-degree object captures, apply the scheme inversely by placing the circumscribed sphere around the object [S3]. Without ray unification, the same ray is encoded differently depending on the starting location, requiring different depth values [S3]. Ray unification adds about 0.6 dB in PSNR compared to no unification [S3].
+A technique to map every ray starting location onto a sphere circumscribed around the view cell, ensuring that identical rays have identical depth values regardless of the camera position, easing the task for the oracle network [S3]. It differs from the parent by post-processing the ray origins to normalize their spatial distribution relative to the view cell [S3]. Choose it when using a depth oracle network that struggles to disambiguate rays from different starting locations [S3]. For 360-degree object captures, apply the scheme inversely by placing the circumscribed sphere around the object [S3]. Without ray unification, the same ray is encoded differently depending on the starting location, requiring different depth values [S3]. Ray unification adds about 0.6 dB in PSNR compared to no unification [S3].
 
 ### Candidate Generation
-A procedure to generate resampling candidates by tracing rays from the camera and sharing the same set of candidates at all texels, avoiding wasteful generation for invisible texels [S4]. Initialize `rs+` and `rs-` as arrays of Reservoirs sized to the texture. For each pixel $j$, loop $k$ from 1 to $m$: sample $x_{j,k}$ from $p_j$. For each texel $i$ where $q_i(x_{j,k}) \neq 0$, update `rs+[i]` with $x_{j,k}$ and weight $\max(q_i(x_{j,k}), 0)/p_j(x_{j,k})$, and `rs-[i]` with $x_{j,k}$ and weight $\max(-q_i(x_{j,k}), 0)/p_j(x_{j,k})$ [S4].
-
-### Batch Stratified Pixel Sampling
-Sample a batch of pixels via stratified sampling at each iteration [S5]. Obtain ground-truth values at sub-pixel coordinates via bilinear interpolation on the target image, implemented as a CUDA texture lookup for efficiency [S5].
+A procedure to generate resampling candidates by tracing rays from the camera and sharing the same set of candidates at all texels, avoiding wasteful generation for invisible texels [S4]. It differs from the parent by focusing on generating a shared set of spatial candidates for importance sampling across all pixels, rather than just generating the primary camera rays [S4]. Choose it when performing parameter-space resampling (e.g., ReSTIR) to improve gradient estimation efficiency [S4]. It initializes reservoirs for each texel and updates them with samples drawn from the pixel rays, weighted by the ratio of target to proposal densities [S4].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{rays})$ for ray generation, where $N_{rays}$ is the number of rays. Matrix operations are $O(H \times W)$ for full grid sampling [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rays})$ to store ray origins and directions [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of rays generated. Matrix operations are $O(H \times W)$ for full grid sampling [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store ray origins and directions [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with the number of rays and image resolution [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple, deterministic (for full grid), and efficient for parallel execution on GPUs [G].
-*   **Limitations:** Does not account for scene content or importance, potentially wasting samples on empty space [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** As a baseline or coarse sampling stage in multi-stage rendering pipelines [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When adaptive sampling is required for variance reduction [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with image resolution and batch size [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple, deterministic (for full grid), and parallelizable [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Does not account for scene geometry or importance; samples empty space uniformly [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Initial ray generation for volume rendering, NeRF training, or as a base for more complex sampling strategies [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When importance sampling is required to reduce variance in sparse scenes [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Grid Ray Sampler is a fundamental component in volume rendering and neural radiance field (NeRF) pipelines, originating from the need to efficiently generate camera rays for rendering [S2, unofficial]. It is commonly used in conjunction with hash encoding and neural networks for scene reconstruction [S2, unofficial]. The method is implemented in various open-source projects, such as SolidVoxelization and cv-master-ex [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+The Grid Ray Sampler is a fundamental component in computer graphics and neural rendering pipelines for generating camera rays from pixel coordinates [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in NeRF-based methods and differentiable rendering frameworks [S1, S2, unofficial][S3, S4]. The method relies on standard pinhole camera models and homogeneous coordinate transformations [S2, unofficial].
 
